Questions:
{
  "questions": [
    {
      "source_id": "1",
      "stem": "A flying squirrel leaps from a tree with initial speed $v_0=12.5\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$ at an angle of $35^\\circ$ away from the vertical (so it is $55^\\circ$ above the horizontal). Neglect air resistance. What is the squirrel\u2019s speed when it reaches its maximum height?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "$12.5\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This assumes the speed stays constant throughout the flight. In ideal projectile motion, only the horizontal component stays constant while the vertical component changes due to gravity."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "$10.2\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This comes from using the wrong trigonometric component (e.g., $v_0\\cos 35^\\circ$), as if the given angle were measured from the horizontal instead of from the vertical."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "$7.2\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "At maximum height the vertical velocity is zero: $v_y=0$. The speed then equals the magnitude of the horizontal component, $v=|v_x|$; since the launch angle is $35^\\circ$ from vertical, $v_x=v_0\\sin 35^\\circ$, so $v=12.5\\sin 35^\\circ\\approx 7.17\\ \\mathrm{m/s}\\approx 7.2\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "$0\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This confuses \u201cvertical velocity is zero at the top\u201d with \u201ctotal velocity is zero.\u201d The horizontal component generally remains nonzero, so the squirrel is not at rest."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_id": "2",
      "stem": "A maglev test cart is moving on a nearly frictionless track with initial velocity $\\vec v_0=(28.0\\ \\mathrm{m/s})\\,\\hat\\imath$. A guidance system then applies a constant acceleration $\\vec a=(1.40\\ \\mathrm{m/s^2})\\,\\hat\\imath+(3.10\\ \\mathrm{m/s^2})\\,\\hat\\jmath$. Which statement is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "The $y$-component of the cart\u2019s velocity remains constant.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This would only be true if $a_y=0$. Here $a_y=3.10\\ \\mathrm{m/s^2}\\neq 0$, so $v_y(t)=v_{y0}+a_y t$ changes with time."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "Both the direction of motion and the speed change.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "With constant acceleration, $\\vec v(t)=\\vec v_0+\\vec a\\,t$, so $v_x(t)=v_{x0}+a_x t$ and $v_y(t)=v_{y0}+a_y t$. Since both $a_x\\neq 0$ and $a_y\\neq 0$, both components of velocity change, which changes the direction (the angle of $\\vec v$) and also changes the speed $|\\vec v|=\\sqrt{v_x^2+v_y^2}$ over time."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The $x$-component of the cart\u2019s velocity remains constant.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This incorrectly assumes $a_x=0$. Because $a_x=1.40\\ \\mathrm{m/s^2}$, the cart\u2019s $x$-velocity must change according to $v_x(t)=v_{x0}+a_x t$."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "None of the above is correct.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This choice overlooks that option B follows directly from the component equations $v_x(t)=v_{x0}+a_x t$ and $v_y(t)=v_{y0}+a_y t$ when both acceleration components are nonzero."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

Create a complete LaTeX document with:
1. A document preamble with necessary packages
2. Custom macros for question formatting (\QBlock, \Correct, \Wrong)
3. All questions formatted using the macros
4. Proper math formatting for all expressions

Set filename to "isomorphic_questions.tex"

